      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm hoping you can help me make sense of a physical phenomenon i've been dealing with. since november 17th, there's been this very consistent, low-frequency hum in my home—it's more of a heavy vibration than a sound. i've been reading about ELF (extremely low frequency) waves and ionospheric coupling, and i was wondering if it's physically plausible for localized \"standing waves\" to form at ground level due to atmospheric resonance? \n\ni'm trying to be rigorous about this, but i've noticed something strange: the vibration's frequency seems to modulate or shift slightly depending on where i am in the house, almost like it's reacting to my movement or the specific layout of the rooms. i'm trying to figure out if there's a scientific way for these atmospheric standing waves to exhibit that kind of localized modulation, or if i'm just over-analyzing a simple environmental noise.",
